WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.140
What do you want to be when you grow up?

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.860
At age four, I wanted to become
a computer scientist.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.020
At five, a vacuum cleaner salesman.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.340
At six, a priest.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.940
At 17, an award-winning filmmaker.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.900
And at 22, when it was finally time
to decide on my first job,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.500
I became a management consultant.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.660
(Laughter)

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.060
What do you want to be when you grow up?

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.620
I'm sure everyone here has been asked
some version of that question,

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.500
but perhaps not recently.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.140
Somehow, the questions have changed.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:39.940
"What do you do for work?"

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.420
"How much money do you make?"

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.580
"Where are you going
on your next vacation?"

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.220
We've become obsessed
with asking these types of questions

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.780
to measure even our nation's productivity.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.020
We track growth rates,
remittances and exports

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.700
without really asking
if these numbers reflect the lives

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.260
that people are trying to live.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.700
I see the problems with this system
at home in the Philippines.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.300
Our GDP is soaring,

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.780
but we're doing it in a way
that doesn't quite align

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.220
with the kind of lives
that people want to live.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.660
For one, we send millions of people abroad

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.460
to take care of other people's
parents and children,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.900
and we train millions more to stay awake
while their families are asleep

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.220
so they can be up all night
answering phone calls

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.660
from halfway across the world

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.820
about how much money
is in their bank account.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.540
And somehow, we're rewarded for it.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.860
We’re told that we’re Southeast Asia’s
second-fastest-growing economy,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.060
and in a few years,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.100
we'll be upgraded to an upper
middle-income country.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.100
As a nation,

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.900
we've become really good
at hitting metrics

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.020
that were never ours to begin with

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.780
and climbing a ladder
that was designed by those

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.900
who were already sitting on top of it.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.660
But what would happen if you asked
Filipinos what metrics mattered to them?

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.540
What would happen if you tried
to measure dreams?

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.500
Last year, my colleagues
and I at BCG ran a nationwide,

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.300
nationally representative survey
to answer this very question.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.020
Now look, no client had ever
asked us about dreams,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.620
but if you look at the websites
and mission statements

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.700
of the country's largest businesses,

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:11.820
all of them are trying
to connect their work

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.740
to national development in some way.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.140
And so we knew that even
if nobody was asking the question,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.380
the answer was one that many people
would be interested in.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.820
We discovered three things.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.940
First, we discovered
what the Filipino dream was.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.100
The number-one ranked dream
across all socioeconomic segments

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.340
was to achieve financial security
to absorb health scares.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.820
But we learned more than that --
we also heard stories.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.380
We heard stories like that of Tidri,
[a] 26-year-old preschool teacher

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.860
who dreams of becoming
a beauty queen, a host,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.220
a politician, opening a hair salon
just like her grandmother.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.980
But right now,
Tidri can't afford to do that

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.380
because she still has to help pay
for her grandfather's healthcare bills

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.020
because he just had a stroke.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.620
Second, we learned
who those dreams are for.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.900
Most Filipinos don't
actually dream for themselves.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.220
They dream for their extended family.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.580
We met people like Joey,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.540
[a] 56-year-old radio technician

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.300
who works from 2am to 11pm most nights.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.940
He lives in a boarding house
close to the station,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.540
given the demands of his job,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.420
but still wants to be a farmer,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.620
a social worker and a high school teacher.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.100
But he really wants to retire.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.980
But every time he asks his company,
they say, "Ah, not yet."

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.940
Right now, he doesn't really have a choice

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.420
because he still has to finish paying
for his daughter's education.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.540
And third, we learned how
people perceived institutions

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.780
as helping them achieve those dreams.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:42.620
We learned that most Filipinos

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.300
actually didn't feel like public
and private institutions

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.220
were helping them achieve their dreams,

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.540
and so they could only rely on themselves.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.300
We met people like Sweden,

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.020
[a] 35-year-old news reporter

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.420
who spent years moving
from one field assignment to another.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.060
But when she had her first child,

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.260
she decided to stay home

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.980
and start a children's clothing
business from her garage,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.780
believing that making and selling
children's clothes online

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.980
would be a better way

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.340
of creating financial
stability for her family.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.420
Today, she dreams of opening up
a flagship store in a mall

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.940
for her business,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.900
and of buying a second home
in the mountains for her family.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.260
Tidri, Joey, Sweden

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.300
and 1,500 other Filipinos
we've interviewed

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:24.260
all still want to be something
when they grow up

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.220
and their dreams seem so achievable.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.180
But somehow to them,
they still feel out of reach.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.900
We shared this report with clients,
the business community, media,

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.500
even government officials.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.140
And every single time
they asked us the question,

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.340
why hasn't this been done before?

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.420
Why haven't we tried to measure dreams?

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:46.620
Look, there are many reasons,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.900
but I think it's because those
who have the power to ask the question

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:51.900
think they already know the answer.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.340
In every single forum
where I've asked the question,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.860
“What do you think is
the number-one Filipino dream,”

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.260
I've never seen the most senior person
in the room answer it correctly.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.900
They usually guess something safe,
like to buy a house

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.500
or something generic,
like to have a happy family.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.140
Less than about five percent
of people guess it

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.020
and it's usually somebody more junior,

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.180
somebody who grew up
in a lower-income family

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.100
and worked their way up the ladder.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.980
And I wonder, in how many
other countries in the world

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.340
is this actually happening?

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.900
Where what we call “development”

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.340
is really just watching some numbers
somewhere going up and to the right,

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.540
but people on the ground
feel nowhere closer

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.580
to where they're actually trying to go
than they were yesterday.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.100
Where the only way up is out.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.340
But wait, we already have something
better than GDP, right?

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.260
Doesn't Gross National Happiness
measure the same thing?

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.620
Look, Bhutan's GNH, I think,
has really redefined development for them.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.380
It's asked whether people are happy,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.020
whether their culture is thriving

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.260
and whether they feel
spiritually fulfilled.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.060
But being happy today

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.180
is very different from feeling
like you're coming closer to your dreams.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:56.260
Bhutan might be one of the most
obsessed countries in the world

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.020
with measuring happiness,

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.900
but about 10 percent of their skilled
and educated population

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:02.740
emigrated in 2022.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.980
Maybe that's the problem
with measuring happiness.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.740
I could be happy today
because I've got everything I need

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.020
and things are going well,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.900
but I might not actually be closer
to where I'm trying to go.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.580
And I think that's what
measuring dreams can do for us.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.980
Look not only at how we feel today,

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:21.060
but at the vector
of that progress over time.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.660
And I don't think what I'm
suggesting is too far off.

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:27.340
After all, most of us here already work
for companies that have HR departments

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.020
that measure the progression
of our employees.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.500
So why couldn't our countries
have HR departments

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:36.820
that measure the personal ambition
and fulfillment of its citizens?

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.580
If every country
has the Department of Defense,

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.780
why couldn't we have
a Department of Dreams?

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:43.940
Imagine this.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.300
The annual Aspiration of the Nation report

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.500
presented by the Secretary of Dreams.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:52.260
It looks at what people dream of by age,
by income group, by geography.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.740
It looks at how people came up
with their dreams,

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.420
how they graduate to bigger ones,

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.660
and when they decide
to give up on old dreams.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.700
Imagine National Dream centers
where you could attend trainings

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.460
on making personal progress,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.500
or social workers trained as life coaches

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:08.660
who can help keep you on track.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.140
Imagine the Department of Dreams
could work with the Department of Health

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.900
to measure how healthcare
emergencies affect ambition.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.020
Could we not improve healthcare outcomes

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.260
if we took a lens on how these emergencies
affect ambition and motivation?

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.900
The Department of Dreams could work
with the Department of Trade and Industry

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.900
to ensure that when people say
they have these dream businesses,

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.980
they actually get the tools
to create them.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:32.620
Or with the Department of Labor,

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.940
to ensure that the jobs
that people say they want

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.020
are the same jobs that are being created.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.940
Or with the Department of Education
to train future students

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:41.500
for those dream jobs.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.900
Look, I understand
that there are free markets,

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.340
but could we not imagine a world
where people said,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:50.140
I want to become a writer or a historian
or an astronaut or a psychiatrist,

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:51.540
and government said,

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.580
"Sure, we'll find a way
to make that happen."

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.940
Imagine how much better the world would be

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:59.940
if everyone was obsessed

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.260
with helping each other
achieve their dreams,

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.540
and somehow there was
a government official somewhere

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.820
accountable for making that happen.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:12.940
There's so much more we still want to do
in our work in measuring dreams.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.020
And we're really just getting started.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.660
For one, we hope to run
this survey every few years

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.940
to see how these dreams change over time.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.780
We also want to run the survey
in other markets

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.940
to see how the Filipino dream
might be different from the Malaysian

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.140
or the Emirati dream,

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.900
and encourage governments
and companies around the world

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.420
to begin taking dreams more seriously.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:34.500
So let's measure dreams.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.340
Let's measure what people dream of

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.300
and how the content
of those dreams changes over time.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:45.340
Let’s measure how much progress people
feel like they’ve made versus last year,

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:49.020
and how much more progress they think
they'll make looking into next year.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:54.180
And then let's measure whether people
perceive institutions as helping them

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.820
or holding them back
from achieving those dreams.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:59.900
Then let's create the conditions

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:03.220
that allow people
to truly live their dreams.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.660
After all,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:09.100
we all still want to be
something when we grow up.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.060
Thank you.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.340
(Applause)

